**Two-Phase Locking (2PL) **** is a concurrency control method used to ensure serializability in database transactions. It divides the execution of a transaction into two phases:

Growing Phase (Acquiring Locks):

The transaction acquires all the locks it needs without releasing any locks.
This includes both shared locks (for reading) and exclusive locks (for writing).
Shrinking Phase (Releasing Locks):

The transaction releases locks and cannot acquire any new locks.
Key Points about 2PL:

Not Just Exclusive Locks:

2PL involves both shared (read) and exclusive (write) locks.
A transaction might acquire several shared locks and then upgrade some to exclusive locks before making changes.
Lock Types:

Shared Lock: Multiple transactions can hold shared locks on the same item simultaneously (for reading).
Exclusive Lock: Only one transaction can hold an exclusive lock on an item (for writing).
Lock Acquisition:

Locks are acquired as needed throughout the growing phase, not all at once at the beginning.
Lock Release:

Locks are typically held until the end of the transaction (in the most common variant, Strict 2PL).
Deadlock Potential:

2PL can lead to deadlocks, which need to be detected and resolved.
Variations:

Strict 2PL: All locks are held until the transaction commits or aborts.
Strong Strict 2PL: Write locks are held until commit/abort, read locks can be released earlier.